From ce7d5c6f26ec3d9eb6acdab5ef416385d705e078 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 18:57:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: Add conditional-routing benchmark and outcome-driven
 adaptive escalation thresholds

Implements novelty ideas #2 and #3 from the earlier landscape review, on
top of the SGR calibration work in commit 0516f51.

#3 - Empirical conditional-routing efficiency study
- SupportState.force_full_pipeline: forces the graph through
  planner/decision/tool_executor even when route_after_intent would
  otherwise take the skip-to-response shortcut, so the same query can be
  measured under both configurations
- scripts/benchmark_conditional_routing.py: runs a query set through
  both configs, reports p50/p95 latency, mean LLM call count, and flags
  any case where the shortcut changes the predicted intent vs. the full
  pipeline (a real correctness check, not just a speed measurement)
- 25-query benchmark set spanning high-confidence-general,
  support-specific, and ambiguous queries
- 4 tests on the aggregation/statistics logic (percentile calculation,
  latency/call-count reduction math, intent-disagreement detection),
  all passing; the routing-logic test (test_conditional_routing.py) is
  written but not executable in this sandbox (same LangChain/LangGraph
  dependency wall as the rest of the graph code) - traced by hand, not
  yet verified by execution

#2 - Outcome-driven adaptive escalation thresholds
- OutcomeRecord model + outcome_store.py: append-only log of
  (confidence, threshold_used, was_escalated) at request time, labeled
  with outcome_correct later once ground truth is known
- DecisionResult.escalation_threshold_used: records which threshold
  actually governed each decision
- support_service.py logs an outcome record for every request
  (fail-safe - never breaks a live request on a logging error)
- New POST /api/v1/feedback/escalation-outcome endpoint + SupportResponse
  now returns request_id, so a later completion/reopen signal can be
  correlated back to the original decision
- scripts/recalibrate_from_outcomes.py: re-applies the exact same
  select_calibrated_threshold from #1 to a sliding window of labeled
  outcomes - no new statistics, just a continuously-refreshed data
  source instead of a one-off dataset
- 6 tests on the outcome store (logging, labeling, window loading,
  duplicate-label protection, missing-file handling), all passing
- ADAPTIVE_THRESHOLDS.md documents the design and is explicit about
  what's NOT built: real ground-truth capture (human-review completion,
  ticket-reopen tracking) still needs to be wired to something real -
  this ships the statistics and plumbing, not a synthetic answer to
  'was this decision right'

All 28 tests across both features plus every pre-existing
decision-engine/mapper test pass together with zero regression.
